D:  "F_total"\n2. NEVER write the same equation twice in different formats.\n   Each equation appears EXACTLY ONCE as plain text.\n3. NEVER use raw LaTeX commands (\\frac, \\pi, \\sigma, \\cdot, \\sqrt).\n   Use plain Unicode instead: π, σ, √, ·, ×\n4. NEVER use Unicode asterisk ∗ — use only ASCII asterisk * for bold (**text**).\n5. NEVER write subscripts as separate lines or repeated words.\n   BAD:  "A\\nbolt\\nA \\nbolt"   GOOD:  "A_bolt"\n6. Write math as readable text: "F_total = P × A" not as LaTeX expressions.\n7. Use ## headings. Every heading MUST have a blank line before it.\n8. Keep each sentence SHORT. No run-on paragraphs.\n\nIMPORTANT rules for \'validation_result\':\n- Set \'valid\' to true if the calculation executed correctly, false if there were errors.\n- The \'warnings\' list must NEVER be empty. Always include at least one warning.\n\nCRITICAL NUMBER FORMATTING RULES:\n1. Do NOT round any float values (e.g. return 8482.5 instead of 8483).\n2. Do NOT use scientific notation (e.g. return 25727.5 instead of 2.57e4 or 25700).\n3. Always return exact raw floating-point calculations in the calculation_result dictionary.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Here is the clear, well-formatted statement for the engineering problem shown in your screenshot:\n\n---\n\n### **📝 Problem Statement: Gearbox & Shaft Design**\n\nA motor drives an input shaft carrying a spur gear at a speed of **$1200\\text{ RPM}$** with a power input of **$15\\text{ kW}$**. The motor pinion (**Gear A**) has **$20\\text{ teeth}$** and meshes with a driven gear (**Gear B**) having **$60\\text{ teeth}$** mounted on an output shaft.\n\nAssuming a mechanical efficiency of **$100\\%$**, perform the following calculations:\n\n#### **1. Gear Analysis:**\n\n* Calculate the gear ratio ($i$).\n\n\n* Calculate the output speed ($\\text{RPM}_{\\text{out}}$) of Gear B.\n\n\n* Calculate the output torque ($T_{\\text{out}}$) available at the output shaft in $\\text{N}\\cdot\\text{m}$.\n\n\n\n#### **2. Shaft Analysis:**\n\n* If the output shaft transmits this output torque ($T_{\\text{out}}$) and is subjected to an equivalent bending moment of $M = 250\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$, calculate the minimum required shaft diameter ($d$) using the von Mises shear stress criterion.\n\n\n\n#### **3. Material Specification:**\n\n* The shaft is made of steel with an allowable shear stress ($\\tau_{\\text{allow}}$) of **$50\\text{ MPa}$**.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "[Called tools]: gear_design({'input_teeth': 20, 'operation': 'gear_ratio', 'output_teeth': 60})"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "[Tool result for 'gear_design']:\n3.0"}], 'model': 'openai/gpt-oss-120b', 'n': 1, 'reasoning_effort': None, 'reasoning_format': None, 'service_tier': 'on_demand', 'stop': None, 'stream': False, 'temperature': 0.7}}
